type: framework
namespace: RBI
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: "RBI Framework for Responsible and Ethical Enablement of AI (FREE-AI)"
source_url: "https://rbidocs.rbi.org.in/rdocs/PublicationReport/Pdfs/FREEAIR130820250A24FF2D4578453F824C72ED9F5D5851.PDF"
source_date: "August 13, 2025"
credit: >
  The Reserve Bank of India FREE-AI Committee Report (August 13, 2025)
  sets out 7 guiding sutras and 26 recommendations for AI in financial
  services, organised under 6 strategic pillars (Infrastructure, Policy,
  Capacity, Governance, Protection, Assurance) across two sub-frameworks
  (Innovation Enablement, Risk Mitigation). This framework file maps the
  subset of recommendations directed at Regulated Entities (REs) — as
  opposed to regulators, industry bodies, or national infrastructure
  initiatives — to Anchor enforcement mechanisms.
verification_note: >
  This version was rebuilt against the verbatim text of the source PDF
  (previously inaccessible; the earlier version of this file mapped
  several rules to INCORRECT recommendation numbers — verified by direct
  extraction of all 26 recommendation texts). Corrections made:
  "AI Liability Framework" is Recommendation 8, not 9. "Board-Approved AI
  Policy" is Recommendation 14, not 6. "Cybersecurity Measures" is
  Recommendation 19, not 18. "AI Inventory" is Recommendation 23, not 24.
  "AI Audit Framework" is Recommendation 24, not 25. "Disclosures" (25)
  and "AI Toolkit" (26) were previously conflated into one rule. Also:
  "CIMS" does not appear anywhere in the source report — the actual named
  mechanism for sector-wide AI risk data is the "EmTech Repository" (via
  RBIH), corrected throughout below.
scope_note: >
  Of the 26 recommendations, roughly half (1-6, 9-13) are directed at
  RBI, other regulators, or industry bodies/SROs — establishing shared
  infrastructure, sandboxes, funding, standing committees, training
  programs, recognition awards. These impose no obligation on an
  individual regulated entity's technical systems and have no
  corresponding Anchor rule, because there is nothing in an RE's
  codebase or audit trail for a scanner to check. Only recommendations
  that impose direct obligations on REs are mapped below.
legal_status: >
  IMPORTANT — LEGAL STATUS: FREE-AI is an advisory committee report, not
  a binding RBI circular, master direction, or notification, as of this
  version's date. RBI has not yet converted these recommendations into
  enforceable regulation. Anchor treats these rules as forward-looking
  best-practice enforcement rather than present binding legal obligation.
  Do not describe RBI-* rules as "mandatory under binding RBI regulation"
  in client-facing material. Update this field if RBI issues a binding
  circular or amends Master Directions to incorporate these recommendations.
layer_2_status: >
  Rules marked obligation_type: provenance, audit, or disclosure depend
  on AnchorRuntime (Layer 2) and the Decision Audit Chain (DAC). Layer 2
  is currently in development. These rules are specified as designed and
  will be enforced once Layer 2 ships.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  - id: "RBI-008"
    name: "AI Liability Framework — Graded, Conditional on Safeguards"
    original_id: "Recommendation 8 (corrected — previously miscited as Recommendation 9)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: demonstrate
      object: safeguard_compliance_for_liability_grading
      context: probabilistic_non_deterministic_ai_failure
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: incident_and_audit_history_to_liability_grading
    anchor_mechanism: >
      chain_hash + signature in AuditEntry provides the non-repudiation
      proof that an RE followed incident reporting, RCA, red teaming, and
      audit safeguards — the exact conditions the report specifies as
      qualifying for a "first instance" tolerant supervisory approach.
      Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      The report proposes a graded liability approach: REs remain fully
      responsible for consumer losses, but a first-time AI failure should
      not automatically trigger full supervisory action if the RE had
      adhered to safeguards — incident reporting, root cause analysis,
      regular red teaming, independent audits, and transparency. This
      tolerant stance is explicitly conditional and does not apply to
      repeated breaches or gross negligence. Anchor's audit chain is the
      technical evidence an RE would present to demonstrate it qualifies
      for this graded treatment — proof the safeguards were actually in
      place, not just claimed after the fact.

  - id: "RBI-014"
    name: "Board-Approved AI Policy"
    original_id: "Recommendation 14 (corrected — previously miscited as Recommendation 6)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: formulate
      object: board_approved_ai_policy
      context: governance_risk_appetite_and_liability_framework
      authority: regulated_entity_board
      flow: policy_to_governance_artifact
    anchor_mechanism: >
      constitution.anchor + policy.anchor as the machine-readable
      board-approved policy artifact. anchor audit --report generates
      the compliance disclosure document.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      REs should establish a board-approved AI policy covering governance
      structure, accountability, risk appetite, operational safeguards,
      auditability, consumer protection measures, AI disclosures, model
      lifecycle framework, and liability framework — with a risk
      classification scheme (low/medium/high) for AI use cases based on
      customer impact and potential for harm. Anchor's sealed
      constitution.anchor and policy.anchor together constitute the
      machine-readable equivalent — cryptographically signed,
      version-controlled, and auditable.

  - id: "RBI-015"
    name: "Data Lifecycle Governance Framework"
    original_id: "Recommendation 15"
    maps_to: "PRV-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: govern
      object: ai_training_and_inference_data
      context: full_data_lifecycle_dpdp_alignment
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: data_source_to_traceable_lineage
    anchor_mechanism: >
      PRV-001 PII leakage detection active in Layer 1. DATA-*
      violation category covers data governance gaps. PROV-003
      provenance violation fires when AI output has no data
      lineage metadata. Layer 1 detection active now.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      REs must establish robust data governance frameworks — internal
      controls and policies for collection, access, usage, retention,
      and deletion of data used in AI systems — compliant with the DPDP
      Act throughout the data lifecycle. Data lineage matters: every AI
      output should be traceable back to the sources that influenced it.
      Not satisfied by policy documents alone — requires technical
      controls demonstrable to a regulator.

  - id: "RBI-016"
    name: "AI System Governance — Model Lifecycle and Autonomous AI Oversight"
    original_id: "Recommendation 16 (previously not covered by any rule)"
    maps_to: "SUP-003"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: govern
      object: ai_model_lifecycle_and_autonomous_agent_actions
      context: design_development_deployment_decommissioning
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: model_metadata_to_drift_and_oversight_detection
    anchor_mechanism: >
      SUP-003 versioning drift violation fires when model version is
      undeclared or inconsistent — the technical substrate for the
      "model inventory and documentation" requirement below. AGT-001
      (agent action authorization bypass) directly covers the
      autonomous-AI human-oversight requirement for medium/high-risk
      use cases. No dedicated rule yet exists for model drift/degradation
      detection specifically (as opposed to version tracking) — flagged
      as a real gap, candidate for a new MODEL-* rule category.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      REs must implement model governance across the full AI lifecycle —
      design, development, deployment, decommissioning — maintaining a
      model inventory with objectives, design features, performance
      benchmarks, and mechanisms to detect model degradation, drift,
      bias, or unexplained behavior with defined fallback mechanisms.
      Separately and significantly: REs deploying autonomous AI systems
      (able to independently execute financial actions — investment
      decisions, loan processing, payment execution) must establish
      clear safeguards, with human oversight mandatory for medium- and
      high-risk autonomous use cases. This is one of the more
      consequential recommendations for agentic AI specifically, and
      currently only partially covered by existing Anchor rules.

  - id: "RBI-017"
    name: "Product Approval Process for AI Features"
    original_id: "Recommendation 17"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: gate
      object: ai_enabled_product_launch
      context: independent_evaluation_team
      authority: internal_governance_sign_off
      flow: ci_cd_check_to_deployment_gate
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor check in CI/CD pipeline acts as the technical gate in the
      product approval process. A failing audit blocks deployment. The
      violation report is the governance sign-off artifact. Layer 1
      active now.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      All AI-enabled products and solutions should be brought within the
      institutional product approval framework, with AI-specific risk
      evaluations covering fairness, bias, understandability, customer
      protection, and cybersecurity across the full product lifecycle.
      The report specifically recommends the approval evaluation team be
      independent from the teams that built and deployed the model —
      running anchor check as a required, independently-owned CI/CD gate
      satisfies this separation-of-duties expectation in practice.

  - id: "RBI-018"
    name: "Consumer Protection — Transparency and Human Escalation"
    original_id: "Recommendation 18 (corrected — previously this number was wrongly used for Cybersecurity)"
    maps_to: ["AGT-002", "ETH-002"]
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: disclose_escalate
      object: ai_customer_interaction
      context: chatbot_and_automated_decision_channels
      authority: consumer
      flow: ai_interaction_to_human_escalation_path
    anchor_mechanism: >
      AGT-002 (agent action outside scope) partially covers this.
      No dedicated rule yet checks for: (a) explicit disclosure that a
      consumer is interacting with AI, (b) an available human escalation
      path, (c) absence of any code path that would let a system falsely
      represent itself as human. Flagged as a real gap — directly
      parallel to CFPB-005 (chatbot compliance risk) added to the CFPB
      framework file; worth building as a shared CHATBOT-* rule category
      usable across both jurisdictions rather than duplicating logic.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      REs should establish a board-approved consumer protection framework
      prioritizing transparency, fairness, and accessible recourse.
      Consumers must be explicitly informed when interacting with AI,
      must always have the option to escalate to a human, and firms must
      not deceive customers by falsely claiming (or failing to disclose)
      AI involvement. This recommendation and CFPB's June 2023 chatbot
      guidance point at the same underlying risk from two jurisdictions —
      a strong candidate for one shared detection rule rather than two
      separate, jurisdiction-specific ones.

  - id: "RBI-019"
    name: "Cybersecurity Measures — AI-Specific Threats"
    original_id: "Recommendation 19 (corrected — previously miscited as Recommendation 18)"
    maps_to: ["SEC-001", "SEC-007"]
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: defend
      object: ai_system_attack_surface
      context: prompt_injection_data_poisoning_model_tampering
      authority: regulated_entity_security_function
      flow: threat_pattern_to_static_detection
    anchor_mechanism: >
      SEC-001 prompt injection, SEC-002 data poisoning, SEC-003 model
      tampering, AGT-003 MCP compromise all fire in Layer 1 static
      analysis. Full coverage of the AI-specific cybersecurity threats
      this recommendation lists under the Protection pillar.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      REs must identify AI-specific security risks — adversarial attacks,
      data poisoning, model manipulation — and strengthen their
      cybersecurity ecosystem accordingly, on a continuous basis rather
      than only pre-deployment. The report also notes AI can strengthen
      cybersecurity itself (anomaly detection, threat intelligence) —
      Anchor's own static analysis engine is an instance of this pattern.

  - id: "RBI-020"
    name: "Red Teaming — Risk-Proportionate, Trigger-Based"
    original_id: "Recommendation 20 (previously not covered by any rule)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditLog"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: adversarially_test
      object: ai_model_and_application
      context: periodic_and_trigger_based
      authority: internal_red_team_or_third_party
      flow: red_team_finding_to_documented_mitigation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      No dedicated Anchor rule currently tracks red-teaming cadence or
      findings. DAC AuditLog could record red-team exercises as a
      distinct entry type once Layer 2 ships. Flagged as a real gap —
      candidate for a new obligation_type: red_team category.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      REs should establish structured, adversarial red-teaming processes
      across the AI lifecycle, with frequency proportional to risk —
      at least semi-annual for medium/high-risk applications, at minimum
      pre-deployment for low-risk ones — plus trigger-based red teaming
      after major model updates, detected vulnerabilities, or environment
      changes. Findings should be documented and accessible to audit/
      supervisory teams.

  - id: "RBI-021"
    name: "Business Continuity Plan for AI Systems"
    original_id: "Recommendation 21 (previously not covered by any rule)"
    maps_to: "OPS-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: recover
      object: ai_specific_failure_mode
      context: distribution_shift_and_silent_degradation
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: failure_detection_to_fallback_mechanism
    anchor_mechanism: >
      OPS-001 (Availability and Denial) already detects missing circuit
      breakers and timeout/overload handling — directly relevant, but
      OPS-001 as currently written targets generic availability risk,
      not the AI-specific failure mode this recommendation describes
      (a model that stays technically "up" but produces silently
      degraded/unreliable output). Worth extending OPS-001 or adding a
      dedicated rule for silent-degradation detection specifically.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      REs must augment existing Business Continuity Plans to cover
      AI-specific failure modes — not just traditional outages, but
      models that remain functional while producing unreliable output
      due to distribution shift or data drift. The report specifically
      calls for a model to be able to declare itself "unavailable" and
      trigger backup processes, plus regular BCP drills simulating drift
      scenarios and periodic human validation sampling (the report
      suggests 1% of decisions) to catch silent degradation.

  - id: "RBI-022"
    name: "AI Incident Reporting and Sectoral Risk Intelligence"
    original_id: "Recommendation 22 (previously not covered by any rule)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: report
      object: ai_related_incident
      context: tiered_entity_sector_national_reporting
      authority: regulator_via_emtech_repository
      flow: incident_to_non_punitive_disclosure
    anchor_mechanism: >
      DAC AuditEntry could be extended with an incident_report flag and
      severity classification feeding an EmTech-Repository-compatible
      export format. Not yet built — Layer 2 in development, and this
      specific export format is a new scope item, not yet started.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Regulators should establish a tiered AI incident reporting
      framework for REs, with a tolerant, good-faith approach that
      encourages timely disclosure rather than punishing it — reporting
      should feed an aggregated risk-intelligence view (the report
      suggests expanding RBIH's existing EmTech Repository for this),
      not just an internal log. Note: this recommendation is explicitly
      addressed to "financial sector regulators" to establish the
      framework, with REs as the reporting party — worth being precise
      that Anchor's role here is enabling an RE's timely, complete
      incident disclosure, not the regulator-side framework itself.

  - id: "RBI-023"
    name: "AI Inventory — Internal and Sector-Wide"
    original_id: "Recommendation 23 (corrected — previously miscited as Recommendation 24)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: inventory
      object: deployed_ai_models_use_cases_and_grievances
      context: semi_annual_update_supervisory_inspection
      authority: rbi_regulator
      flow: model_metadata_to_live_inventory
    anchor_mechanism: >
      model_version + model_id in every AuditEntry constitutes the AI
      inventory record per decision. SUP-003 versioning drift violation
      fires when model version is undeclared or inconsistent. The
      report's inventory scope also includes third-party/dependency
      tracking and a grievance record per AI system — dependency
      tracking exists via SUP-003; grievance-record linkage does not yet
      exist as a distinct field. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      REs should maintain a comprehensive internal AI inventory — models,
      algorithms, use cases, dependencies, risk categorization, and a
      record of grievances filed against each AI system — updated at
      least semi-annually and available for supervisory inspection.
      Separately, regulators should build a sector-wide, anonymized AI
      repository (extending RBIH's EmTech Repository) to track adoption
      trends and systemic concentration risk across the sector — that
      second part is a regulator-side initiative Anchor doesn't act on
      directly.

  - id: "RBI-024"
    name: "AI Audit Framework — Risk-Based, Three-Tier"
    original_id: "Recommendation 24 (corrected — previously miscited as Recommendation 25)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditLog"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: audit
      object: ai_input_data_model_and_output_behavior
      context: risk_calibrated_internal_third_party_and_periodic
      authority: internal_and_independent_auditors
      flow: risk_level_to_audit_intensity
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor audit command produces the internal audit artifact,
      corresponding to the report's "Internal Audits" tier. DAC
      verify_chain() provides tamper-evident audit chain for third-party
      auditors, corresponding to the "Third-Party Audits" tier for
      high-risk/complex use cases. anchor audit --report generates the
      artifact for the "Periodic Review" tier (report specifies at least
      biennial). Layer 2 in development for full DAC audit support.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      REs should implement a comprehensive, risk-based AI audit
      framework covering (a) internal audits proportionate to risk
      level, (b) independent third-party audits for high-risk/complex
      use cases, and (c) periodic review of the audit framework itself,
      at least biennially, to incorporate emerging risks. The audit
      should verify input data quality, model/algorithm integrity, and
      output/behavior explainability and fairness — matching Anchor's
      own audit trail, DAC chain, and violation-report artifacts closely
      enough that this is one of the most directly implementable
      recommendations in the report.

  - id: "RBI-025"
    name: "AI Disclosures in Annual Reports"
    original_id: "Recommendation 25 (corrected — previously conflated with Recommendation 26 under one rule)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: disclose
      object: ai_governance_framework_and_safeguards
      context: annual_report_and_website
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: audit_report_to_public_disclosure
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor audit --report generates the JSON and Markdown compliance
      report feeding annual disclosure requirements. The sealed
      constitution.anchor SHA-256 hash (and, once real Ed25519 signing is
      enforced end-to-end, the GOVERNANCE.lock signature) provides
      cryptographic attestation of the compliance toolkit's integrity.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      REs should include AI-related disclosures in annual reports and on
      their websites. The report draws an explicit parallel to how
      climate-risk and cybersecurity disclosures became standard ESG/
      annual-report features — AI disclosure is positioned to follow the
      same path. Regulators are expected to specify a standard disclosure
      framework for consistency across institutions.

  - id: "RBI-026"
    name: "AI Compliance Toolkit — Standardised Validation"
    original_id: "Recommendation 26 (corrected — previously conflated with Recommendation 25 under one rule)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: validate_benchmark
      object: ai_model_against_responsible_ai_principles
      context: voluntary_industry_maintained_toolkit
      authority: recognised_sro_or_industry_body
      flow: model_output_to_compliance_benchmark
    anchor_mechanism: >
      Anchor itself is a candidate instance of the "AI Compliance
      Toolkit" the report envisions — third-party, validates against
      fairness/transparency/accountability/robustness dimensions. Worth
      noting explicitly in positioning: this recommendation describes
      almost exactly the product category Anchor occupies.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Regulators should facilitate industry-led AI Compliance Toolkits
      — developed and maintained by a recognised SRO or industry body —
      that let REs validate and benchmark their AI models against
      fairness, transparency, accountability, and robustness. Use should
      be voluntary but strongly encouraged, especially for smaller REs
      lacking internal capability. Notably, the report explicitly
      anticipates third-party toolkit providers, without regulatory
      endorsement — a directly relevant positioning point for Anchor.
